# Coder template conventions

Apply this rule only to Terraform that declares the `coder/coder`
provider. Ignore it for other Terraform.

For scaffolding, pulling, pushing, and testing, follow the `templates`
skill. For adding registry modules, follow the `modules` skill.

- Use `data "coder_parameter"` for choices the workspace user makes
  (image, CPU, memory, repository). Use a Terraform `variable` for
  choices the admin fixes at push time (architecture, cluster, storage
  pool). Never expose infrastructure details as parameters.
- Give each `coder_parameter` a `display_name` and `description`. Set
  `mutable = true` only when changing the value on rebuild is safe. Use
  `option` blocks for enums and `validation` blocks for ranges and
  patterns.
- Define one `coder_agent` per machine. Do not set the deprecated `dir`
  argument; the agent starts in `$HOME`.
- Set `count = data.coder_workspace.me.start_count` on compute that
  should stop with the workspace. Keep persistent storage (the home
  volume or disk) outside that `count`, and name it from
  `data.coder_workspace.me.id` so it survives stop and start.
- Pin `version` on every `registry.coder.com` module and pass the
  agent with `agent_id`.
- Never put credentials, OAuth secrets, or tokens in `.tf` or
  `.tfvars` files. Mark sensitive variables `sensitive = true` and
  rely on the provisioner's own cloud authentication.
- Before pushing, run `terraform fmt` and `terraform validate`, and
  offer the `coder-template-reviewer` subagent for a review.
- Do not pass `--activate` to `coder templates push` while the user is
  still iterating.
